{
  "bridge_text": "Three hundred and ninety-one steps would be a long way to climb, but the stairs before Lina and Mosk were just winding down into the dark. With the apple left behind on the step, they continued their descent into the quiet humming below.",
  "resume_strategy": "restart",
  "replacement_segments": [
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "text": "Mosk pointed at the door, his wooden claw clicking against the frame. Lina reached into her pocket and pulled out the last apple of the season, placing it on the step as a thank you for the long journey. Mosk stared at the fruit, perhaps counting the seeds or just remembering the light of the sun, before he stood up and began to descend the stairs."
    }
  ],
  "active_scene_id": "s3"
}

{
  "bridge_text": "The firefly's light dims as if she were a candle in a drafty room, leaving the hollow tree filled with a heavy, quiet stillness. We must see if the medicine they bring can kindle that spark once more.",
  "resume_strategy": "continue",
  "replacement_segments": [
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "text": "She had been his friend once, he whispered, and one winter long ago he had failed to bring her medicine in time. He had been guarding the tree ever since, ashamed to face her. Now, as her light flickered like a dying star, he looked to Lina with wide, pleading eyes, hoping that perhaps this time the story would have a kinder ending for his old friend."
    }
  ],
  "active_scene_id": "s4"
}

{
  "bridge_text": "Patience is the secret ingredient to any good magic, and yours is just beginning to bloom. With the frosty field behind her, Lina stepped toward the tree's silent invitation.",
  "resume_strategy": "skip",
  "replacement_segments": [
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "text": "Inside the hollow tree she found a curl of stairs going down, and at the very top step sat an old grey fox in a patched green vest. His name was Mosk. He told her, in a voice like dry leaves, that the tree was not for visiting and that she should go home and forget the humming. Lina stood her ground, whispering that she could not forget what she had not yet heard."
    },
    {
